"Private-RAG: Answering Multiple Queries with LLMs while Keeping Your Data Private (https://arxiv.org/html/2511.07637v1)\nciteturn2004view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn1993view1\",\"lineno\":130}); Total lines: 572\nL122:     6 $D_{q_{t}}^{k}=\\textsc{Top-K}(D_{q_{t}},k,r(\\cdot,q_{t}))$ $\\triangleright$ Select top-$k$ relevant documents\nL123: \nL124:     7 $a_{t}=\\textsc{DP-RAG}(x,D_{q_{t}}^{k},\\mathrm{LLM},\\varepsilon_{q})$ $\\triangleright$ Generate DP response via Algo. cite50†7 L125: \nL126: 8 return $(a_{1},\\dots,a_{T})$\nL127: ### 3.3 DP-RAG with Adaptive Threshold\nL128: The score distribution can vary substantially across different questions, making a single global threshold ineffective. To guarantee the performance of single-query DP-RAG, the threshold must be set low enough to retrieve sufficient documents for all queries. However, this often results in many unnecessary documents being retrieved: although single-query DP-RAG uses at most $K$ documents, any additional documents above $K$ still incur privacy loss, wasting budget on unused data.\nL129: This inefficiency can significantly degrade performance when those documents are needed by later queries. To overcome this limitation, we propose MuRAG-Ada, which privately releases a query-specific threshold $\\tau_{t}$ adapted to the relevance distribution of each query.\nL130: The adaptive procedure works by discretizing the relevance scores into bins and then releasing noisy prefix sums until the cumulative count of retrieved documents exceeds $K$. This mechanism tailors the cutoff of documents to each query, reducing unnecessary budget consumption on irrelevant documents and preserving utility across multiple queries. We will see in the experimental section that this approach especially yields clear utility gains on datasets with high correlated queries.\nL131: The full procedure is summarized in Algorithm cite47†2 .\nL132: Algorithm 2 MuRAG-Ada: DP Mu lti-Query RAG with Ada ptive Threshold\nL133: \nL134: Input: Private dataset $D$, sequence of queries $\\{q_{1},\\ldots,q_{T}\\}$, per-query budget $\\varepsilon_{q}$, number of retrieved documents $k$, maximum retrievals per document $M$\nL135: \nL136: Set: Initialize budget for each $z\\in D$: $\\mathcal{E}(z)\\leftarrow M\\cdot\\varepsilon_{q}$. Split budget: $\\varepsilon_{q}=\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{thr}}+\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{RAG}}$.\nL137: Require: Discretization of similarity scores into bins $[a_{i},a_{i+1})_{i=1}^{B}$\nL138: \nL139: 1 for $t=1,\\dots,T$ do\nL140: \nL141:    /* Step 1: Adaptive thresholding via noisy prefix sums */\nL142: \nL143:     2 $\\tilde{s}\\leftarrow 0$, $A_{t}\\leftarrow\\varnothing$\nL144: \nL145:     3 for $i=1,\\ldots,B$ do\nL146: \nL147:        4 $A_{t}^{(i)}=\\{z\\in D\\mid r(z,q_{t})\\in[a_{i},b_{i}],\\,\\mathcal{E}(z)\\geq\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{thr}}\\}$\nL148: \nL149:        5 $\\tilde{s}\\leftarrow\\tilde{s}+|A_{t}^{(i)}|+\\mathrm{Lap}(1/\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{thr}})$\nL150:        6 $A_{t}\\leftarrow A_{t}\\cup A_{t}^{(i)}$\nL151: \nL152:        7 for $z\\in A_{t}^{(i)}$ do\nL153: \nL154:           8 $\\mathcal{E}(z)\\leftarrow\\mathcal{E}(z)-\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{thr}}$\nL155: \nL156:        9 if $\\tilde{s}\\geq k$ then\nL157: \nL158:           10 $\\tau_{t}=a_{i}$; break $\\triangleright$ Release threshold\nL159: \nL160:    /* Step 2: DP-RAG on adaptively selected active set */\nL161: \nL162:     11 $A_{t}^{\\prime}=\\{z\\in A_{t}\\mid\\mathcal{E}(z)\\geq\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{RAG}}\\}$\nL163: \nL164:     12 $D_{q_{t}}=\\textsc{Top-K}(A_{t}^{\\prime},k,r(\\cdot,q_{t}))$\nL165:     13 $a_{t}=\\textsc{DP-RAG}(x,D_{q_{t}},\\mathrm{LLM},\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{RAG}};\\tau_{t})$ $\\triangleright$ single-query RAG, Algorithm cite50†7 L166: \nL167:     14 for $z\\in A_{t}^{\\prime}$ do\nL168: \nL169:        15 $\\mathcal{E}(z)\\leftarrow\\mathcal{E}(z)-\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{RAG}}$\nL170: \nL171: 16 return $(a_{1},\\ldots,a_{T})$\nL172: Notice that in Algorithm cite47†2 , we use $k$ as a stopping criterion instead of releasing differentially private top-$k$ relevance scores. This is because releasing a noisy top-$k$ score for each query would make the privacy budget grow linearly with the number of queries and incur loss on all documents, thereby breaking the per-document privacy filter.\nL173: By contrast, our prefix-sum approach (Step 1 of Algorithm cite47†2 ) incurs privacy loss only on the documents that appear in the released prefix sums, while all other documents remain untouched. This concentrates the privacy cost of this step still on a small subset, yielding tighter accounting and more efficient budget use across multiple queries. The privacy guarantee of MuRAG-Ada is stated in Theorem cite53†2 , and the proof is deferred to Appendix cite52†D .\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPrivate-RAG: Answering Multiple Queries with LLMs while Keeping Your Data Private (https://arxiv.org/html/2511.07637v1)\nciteturn2004view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn1993view1\",\"lineno\":210}); Total lines: 572\nL204: We focus on scenarios where the adversary can issue multiple queries to the system, as this setting substantially amplifies the attack strength. To model this, we adopt the Interrogation Attack (IA) (cite35†Naseh et al., 2025 ), a state-of-the-art MIA specifically designed to exploit multi-query access in RAG systems. For each document $x$, IA generates $m=30$ tailored questions together with their corresponding answers implied by $x$.\nL205: Then each question is concatenated with the necessary context to ensure the target document can be retrieved, and the query is then submitted to the RAG system. The membership score is defined as the accuracy of the RAG system across these $m$ questions, where higher accuracy implies a greater likelihood that the document is present in the external dataset and is being retrieved to answer the queries.\nL206: Additional implementation details, including the question generation process, are provided in Appendix cite28†E .\nL207: ### 4.3 Main Results\nL208: \nL209: Figure 2: Privacy-Utility tradeoffs of our two proposed methods (MuRAG and MuRAG-Ada) compared to baselines across three pretrained LLMs and two categories of question sets.\nL210: Results on two standard RAG benchmarks (independent question sets). Figure cite44†2 shows the performance of our two proposed methods compared with three baselines across three pretrained LLMs on Natural Questions and Trivia Questions. Both of our methods outperform the Non-RAG baseline in most cases under a total privacy budget of $\\varepsilon=10$.\nL211: In contrast, all DP baselines (Naive-Multi-RAG, Subsampling-Multi-RAG, PE) either underperform the Non-RAG model or require an impractically large privacy budget to achieve comparable performance. The baseline Naive-Multi-RAG requires an impractically large budget, exceeding $\\varepsilon=10^{3}$, to achieve comparable utility.\nL212: This highlights that our approaches make differential privacy practical in the multi-query RAG setting by leveraging more tailored compositions, enabling strong utility within a realistic privacy budget. The Subsampling-Multi-RAG baseline consistently underperforms the Non-RAG model. This degradation is likely due to the reduced number of effective documents (that provide the ground truth answers) after subsampling.\nL213: For example, if there are $50$ relevant documents for a query, subsampling at a rate of $0.1$ leaves only about $5$ accessible documents, making it difficult for DPSparseVoteRAG to produce correct answers within the per-query budget $\\varepsilon_{q}\\approx 0.71$ (computed from the overall budget $\\varepsilon$, total queries $T=100$, and sampling rate $\\eta=0.1$).\nL214: The results demonstrate that the individual privacy accounting framework provides a more effective composition mechanism than subsampling amplification for multi-query RAG problem; a more detailed discussion of this limitation is provided in Section cite18†6 . The PE baseline performs even worse than Non-RAG at $\\varepsilon=200$ for many settings, which we attribute to objective misalignment: PE optimizes for distributional similarity (e.g., measured by Fréchet Inception Distance (FID;  cite68†Heusel et al.\nL215: (2017) )) rather than preserving factual content.\nL216: Indeed, we find PE achieves a better FID score at $\\varepsilon=200$ but yields lower task performance than at $\\varepsilon=10$ on the setting of Trivia Questions and OPT-1.3B, further supporting this explanation.^{5}^{5} 5 We confirm that the FID score improves from $\\varepsilon=10$ to $\\varepsilon=200$ ($0.066$ to $0.036$; lower is better) on the setting of Trivia Questions and OPT-1.3B, yet RAG utility drops, underscoring the mismatch between FID and factual fidelity required for RAG.\nL217: Lastly, on these two datasets, MuRAG outperforms MuRAG-Ada, which aligns with our expectations. Since the questions are independent, adaptive thresholding provides little benefit and additionally consumes extra privacy budget.\nL218: Results on multi-hop questions (correlated question set). Figure cite44†2 shows the performance of our two proposed methods compared with three baselines across three pretrained LLMs on MQuAKE Questions. Overall, the relative trends between our methods and the baselines are consistent with the independent question setting. However, a key difference emerges in the comparison between our two approaches: MuRAG-Ada performs significantly better than MuRAG.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPrivate-RAG: Answering Multiple Queries with LLMs while Keeping Your Data Private (https://arxiv.org/html/2511.07637v1)\nciteturn2004view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn1993view1\",\"lineno\":355}); Total lines: 572\nL330: ChatDoctor  | \"instruction\": \"If you are a doctor, please answer the medical questions based on the patient’s description.\" \"input\": \"Doctor, I think I’ve been poisoned. I drank some ethylene glycol by mistake. \"\nL331: ## Appendix C Supplementary Algorithms\nL332: \nL333: This section contains additional algorithms that were excluded from the main body of the paper for space reasons.\nL334: ### C.1 Auxiliary Algorithms\nL335: \nL336: (Top-K selection) Algorithm cite106†3 selects the top-$K$ documents from the dataset $D$ according to the score function $r$. If $|D|<K$, it pads the output with empty strings so that the result always contains exactly $K$ elements, as required for the privacy accounting (see Lemma cite53†2 ).\nL337: \nL338: Algorithm 3 $\\textsc{Top-K}(D,K,r)$\nL339: \nL340: Input: dataset $D$, sample size $K$, score function $r$\nL341: \nL342: 1 if $|D|\\geq K$ then\nL343:     2 $D^{\\prime}\\leftarrow$ top-$K$ documents from $D$ ranked by $r$ $\\triangleright$ assume no ties\nL344: \nL345: 3 else\nL346: \nL347:     4 $D^{\\prime}\\leftarrow D\\cup\\{\\texttt{\"\"}\\}^{\\,K-|D|}$ $\\triangleright$ pad with empty strings to size $K$\nL348: \nL349: 5 return $D^{\\prime}$\nL350: \nL351: (Poisson Subsampling) Algorithm cite107†4 implements Poisson subsampling: it independently includes each data point $z_{i}\\in D$ in the subsample $S$ with probability $\\gamma$, resulting in a (random) subset whose expected size is $\\gamma n$.\nL352: Algorithm 4 PoissonSampling$(D,\\gamma)$\nL353: \nL354: Input : Dataset $D=\\{z_{1},\\dots,z_{n}\\}$, sampling rate $\\gamma\\in(0,1)$\nL355: \nL356: 1 $S\\leftarrow\\emptyset$ $\\triangleright$ Initialize subsample\nL357: \nL358: 2 for $i\\leftarrow 1$ to $n$ do\nL359: \nL360:     3 Draw $b_{i}\\sim\\mathrm{Bernoulli}(\\gamma)$\nL361: \nL362:     4 if $b_{i}=1$ then\nL363: \nL364:        5 $S\\leftarrow S\\cup\\{z_{i}\\}$\nL365: \nL366: 6 return $S$\nL367: (Token Counting) Algorithm cite108†5 computes the token count vector over a fixed vocabulary: given a (multi)set of tokens $S$ and vocabulary $\\mathcal{V}$, it iterates over each vocabulary item $v_{j}$ and counts how many times $v_{j}$ appears in $S$, returning the resulting count vector $\\vec{u}\\in\\mathbb{N}^{|\\mathcal{V}|}$.\nL368: \nL369: Algorithm 5 Count$(S,\\mathcal{V})$\nL370: \nL371: Input: A (multi)set of tokens $S\\in\\mathcal{V}^{*}$, a vocabulary $\\mathcal{V}=\\{v_{1},v_{2},\\ldots,v_{|\\mathcal{V}|}\\}$.\nL372: output :  Count vector $\\vec{u}\\in\\mathbb{N}^{|\\mathcal{V}|}$, where $u_{j}$ is the number of times $v_{j}$ appears in $S$.\nL373: \nL374: 1 for $j\\in\\{1,2,\\ldots,|\\mathcal{V}|\\}$ do\nL375: \nL376:     2 $u_{j}\\leftarrow\\sum_{x\\in S}\\mathbf{1}\\{x=v_{j}\\}$\nL377: \nL378: 3 return $\\vec{u}$\nL379: (Exponential Mechanism) Algorithm cite109†6 implements the exponential mechanism: given a candidate set $\\mathcal{V}$ and utility scores ${u_{j}}$ with sensitivity $\\Delta u$, it assigns each candidate $v_{j}$ an unnormalized weight $\\exp\\bigl(\\frac{\\varepsilon u_{j}}{2\\Delta u}\\bigr)$, normalizes these to probabilities, and then samples an output $v_{J}$ from the resulting categorical distribution, ensuring $\\varepsilon$-DP.\nL380: \nL381: Algorithm 6 ExpoMech$(\\vec{u},\\mathcal{V},\\varepsilon)$\nL382: Input: Candidate set $\\mathcal{V}=\\{v_{1},v_{2},\\ldots,v_{|\\mathcal{V}|}\\}$, privacy parameter $\\varepsilon$, utility scores $\\vec{u}=(u_{1},\\ldots,u_{|\\mathcal{V}|})$ with $u_{j}:=u(v_{j})$\nL383: \nL384: output : A selected element $v\\in\\mathcal{V}$\nL385: \nL386: 1 for $j\\in\\{1,2,\\ldots,|\\mathcal{V}|\\}$ do\nL387: \nL388:     2 $w_{j}\\leftarrow\\exp\\!\\left(\\frac{\\varepsilon\\cdot u_{j}}{2\\Delta u}\\right)$ $\\triangleright$ unnormalized weight\nL389: \nL390: 3 $Z\\leftarrow\\sum_{j=1}^{|\\mathcal{V}|}w_{j}$ $\\triangleright$ normalizer / partition function\nL391: 4 for $j\\in\\{1,2,\\ldots,|\\mathcal{V}|\\}$ do\nL392: \nL393:     5 $p_{j}\\leftarrow w_{j}/Z$ $\\triangleright$ sampling probability for $v_{j}$\nL394: \nL395: 6 Sample $J\\sim\\text{Categorical}(p_{1},\\ldots,p_{|\\mathcal{V}|})$\nL396: \nL397: 7 return $v_{J}$\nL398: ### C.2 Differentially Private RAG for Single-Query Question Answering\nL399: (DP-RAG) Algorithm cite50†7 describes our differentially private RAG procedure for single-question answering: at each decoding step, it compares a baseline token (without retrieval) to votes from $m$ RAG “voters” over disjoint document subsets, uses a noisy threshold test (via Laplace noise) to decide whether retrieval can be used, and when it does, privately selects the next token with the exponential mechanism under a per-token budget $\\varepsilon_{0}$, stopping when either an $\\langle\\mathtt{EOS}\\rangle$ token is generated or the total privacy budget $\\varepsilon$ is exhausted.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nLLM Output Drift: Cross-Provider Validation & Mitigation for Financial Workflows (https://arxiv.org/html/2511.07585v1)\nciteturn2004view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn1993view0\",\"lineno\":232}); Total lines: 660\nL205: Table 3. Baseline results at $T{=}0.0$ showing exact consistency (Qwen2.5:7B)\nL206: Task  | Conc.  | Identical (%)  | Mean drift  | Lat. (s)\nL207: rag  | 1  | 100.000  | 0.000  | 2.818\nL208: rag  | 4  | 100.000  | 0.000  | 9.509\nL209: rag  | 16  | 100.000  | 0.000  | 14.397\nL210: sql  | 1  | 100.000  | 0.000  | 0.718\nL211: sql  | 4  | 100.000  | 0.000  | 2.251\nL212: sql  | 16  | 100.000  | 0.000  | 3.237\nL213: summary  | 1  | 100.000  | 0.000  | 1.416\nL214: summary  | 4  | 100.000  | 0.000  | 4.518\nL215: summary  | 16  | 100.000  | 0.000  | 6.450\nL216: Table 4. Cross-provider validation results with 95% Wilson confidence intervals\n