 4  | 93.750  | 0.044  | 10.867\nL242: rag  | 0.200  | 16  | 56.250  | 0.311  | 16.157\nL243: sql  | 0.000  | 1  | 100.000  | 0.000  | 0.718\nL244: sql  | 0.000  | 4  | 100.000  | 0.000  | 2.251\nL245: sql  | 0.000  | 16  | 100.000  | 0.000  | 3.237\nL246: sql  | 0.200  | 1  | 100.000  | 0.000  | 0.717\nL247: sql  | 0.200  | 4  | 100.000  | 0.000  | 2.296\nL248: sql  | 0.200  | 16  | 100.000  | 0.000  | 3.289\nL249: summary  | 0.000  | 1  | 100.000  | 0.000  | 1.416\nL250: summary  | 0.000  | 4  | 100.000  | 0.000  | 4.518\nL251: summary  | 0.000  | 16  | 100.000  | 0.000  | 6.450\nL252: summary  | 0.200  | 1  | 100.000  | 0.000  | 1.228\nL253: summary  | 0.200  | 4  | 100.000  | 0.000  | 4.016\nL254: summary  | 0.200  | 16  | 100.000  | 0.000  | 5.606\nL255: Note: Complete dataset (n=16 per condition, 480 total runs) across all temperature and concurrency combinations. Architecture matters more than scale: smaller models (7-8B) outperform larger models (120B) in deterministic behavior. RAG tasks most sensitive to configuration; SQL generation remains robust.\nL256: \nL257: Table 6. Model Performance by Task Type at T=0.0\nL258: Model  | RAG  | SQL  | Summary  | Overall Rating\nL259: Tier 1: Excellence across all tasks\nL260: Granite-3-8B  | 100%  | 100%  | 100%  | Excellent\nL261: Qwen2.5:7B  | 100%  | 100%  | 100%  | Excellent\nL262: Tier 2: Task-dependent performance\nL263: Llama-3.3-70B  | 75%  | 100%  | 100%  | Good\nL264: Mistral-Medium  | 56%  | 100%  | 87%  | Fair\nL265: Tier 3: Unsuitable for regulatory use\nL266: GPT-OSS-120B  | 12.5%  | 12.5%  | 12.5%  | Poor\nL267: These results reveal extreme model-dependent variability in deterministic behavior, fundamentally challenging assumptions about LLM consistency in financial applications.\nL268: Our tiered classification framework (Table cite102†10 , Appendix E) categorizes models based on their compliance viability: Tier 1 models (Granite-3-8B, Qwen2.5:7B) achieve perfect determinism at $T{=}0.0$, while Tier 3 models like GPT-OSS-120B exhibit significant nondeterminism with only 12.5% consistency across all tasks and temperatures-including $T{=}0.0$.\nL269: This suggests fundamental architectural differences in inference implementation that render some models unsuitable for financial compliance regardless of configuration.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nProcedural Knowledge Improves Agentic LLM Workflows (https://arxiv.org/pdf/2511.07568v1)\nciteturn2004view4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 weeks ago; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 43; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn1996view1\",\"lineno\":365}); Total lines: 1888\nL345@P8: is empty, or there are no relevant methods for head(K).\nL346@P8: After calling UpdateTask, ProcLLM then processes a sequence of tasks K up to the horizon H (Line 3)\nL347@P8: or until K is empty (Line 4). Line 6 assembles an action context Ca from the current environment\nL348@P8: state s, the execution trace of the last system execution, and the current task κ. Ca is processed by\nL349@P8: action-LLM (Line 7) which produces a parameterized action a, which is either an internal verify action\nL350@P8: or an external read/write/append action. For a verify action (Lines 9-15), Line 10 assembles a verify\nL351@P8: context Cv from the system state and current task, which is processed by the verify-LLM (Line 11).\nL352@P8: If there remain tasks to complete, Lines 16-22 execute external actions in the environment. These\nL353@P8: external actions are JSON output that is parsed into an action a. For this paper, a set of python scripts\nL354@P8: implements the transition T (s,a)), which produces a new system state a that is fed into the agent for the\nL355@P8: next cycle.\nL356@P8: 5 Empirical Evaluation\nL357@P8: We evaluate ProcLLM on two benchmarks with external APIs and two with a combinatorial solution\nL358@P8: space often solved with search, summarized in the table below. Travel Planning (TP), based on\nL359@P8: (33), requires booking a single-city itinerary using several tools. Recipe Generator (RG), a synthetic\nL360@P8: problem, requires proposing a recipe from a list of ingredients using a tool and solver. Blocks World\nL361@P8: (BW), a planning benchmark (28), requires rearranging stacks of blocks. Unit Movement (UM),\nL362@P8: another synthetic benchmark, requires moving units around a graph.\nL363@P8: Solver? Tools? Problem Type Source\nL364@P8: TP Y Y Tool Use (33)\nL365@P8: RG Y Y Algorithmic Tool Use This paper\nL366@P8: BW Planning (28)\nL367@P8: UM Game This paper\nL368@P8: The agent is given a horizon of H = 100. Solutions are automatically verified using human-written\nL369@P8: simulators and test functions. We test three conditions: human-created M (Human-TN), LLM-created\nL370@P8: M (LLM-TN), or M = ∅ (No-TN). For No-TN, K has one task describing the end conditions for solving the\nL371@P8-9: 9Table 1: Comparison: Mean success rate on subtasks in reduced Travel Planner benchmark (Verification\nL372@P9: set from (33))\nL373@P9: Model Flight 1 Flight 2 Hotel\nL374@P9: Nemotron 70b No-TN 0.023 0.0 0.0\nL375@P9: Nemotron 70b Human-TN 0.535 0.419 0.116\nL376@P9: GPT-oss 120b No-TN 0.0 0.0 0.0\nL377@P9: GPT-oss 120b Human-TN 0.814 0.605 0.395\nL378@P9: problem instance; it functions similar to a vanilla reflexion agent (23). Experiments are performed on an\nL379@P9: AMD EPYC 7H12 64 core CPU with the LLMs being served through ollama hosted on A100 GPUs.\nL380@P9: Travel Planning. This tool-use problem is based on the TravelPlanner benchmark (33), where the agent\nL381@P9: can access database APIs (FlightSearch, RestaurantSearch, etc.) to create an itinerary satisfying a\nL382@P9: user request. The original benchmark contains very difficult multi-constraint tasks where GPT-4 had a\nL383@P9: low 0.6% success rate (33). Although other works have claimed higher success rates on this benchmark,\nL384@P9: they require considerable human-written code scaffolding (10) or they use a simplified version of this\nL385@P9: benchmark (e.g., eliminating tool use in the case of (9)).\nL386@P9: We use several simplifications so task completion is within reach of current models while keeping\nL387@P9: success non-trivial. First, we filter the validation set of (33) to obtain single destination travel requests.\nL388@P9: Second, we used a subset of the original benchmark: book a flight from origin to destination, book\nL389@P9: accommodation, and book a return flight. This subtask benchmark remains sufficiently difficult. Request\nL390@P9: is filled with a user request from the validation set of (33). ProblemSpec specifies that the agent should\nL391@P9: only fulfill parts of the request in order (in contrast to (33)).\nL392@P9: We created 43 problems to solve for TP. Table 1 shows the success rate for completing the task up\nL393@P9: to a given column. For example, for Flight 2, Nemotron with Human-TN chose correct departing and\nL394@P9: returning flights in 41.9% of the requests. For both Nemotron and GPT-oss, Human-TN substantially\nL395@P9: improves task success rate over No-TN.\nL396@P9: Recipe Generator (RG). In this tool use task, an agent is given a list of cooking ingredients and\nL397@P9: asked to return a dish that can be made from these ingredients. To process user requests, the agent\nL398@P9: uses two python functions: get_ingredient_from_dish(dish) returns the ingredients for a dish, and\nL399@P9: get_dish_from_ingredient(ingredient) returns a list of dishes for an ingredient. verify succeeds for\n"
